    "claim": "Popper's preference for the better-corroborated theory is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference that the theory is more likely true, but as a methodological rule to select the theory with the highest empirical content and thus the greatest capacity for future falsification; accepting this costs us the ability to claim rational assurance that the chosen theory will succeed in practice, reducing scientific action to a non-justified 'leap' guided only by the aim of maximizing testability.",
    "mechanism": "Reframe 'preference' from a truth-tracking probability estimate to a risk-maximizing strategy: the scientist chooses the theory that has survived the severest tests because it is the most informative (least probable a priori) and therefore offers the best opportunity to learn something new if it fails next. This avoids induction by refusing to project past success into future reliability, instead projecting it into future vulnerability.",
